  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   Central idea: This approach leverages frequency-domain analysis to discover patterns in the optimal function that minimize maximum correlation. Instead of optimizing in the time domain directly, we transform the problem into the frequency domain and explore solution landscapes across scales and harmonies. If successful, this method may capture global structure in the optimal function that eludes time-domain optimization alone.\n   \n   Advantages:\n   - Frequency analysis can reveal structural properties invisible in time domain\n   - Multi-resolution optimization finds patterns at different scales\n   - Radial symmetry detection may uncover fundamental patterns\n   - Fourier features explicitly guide optimization toward minimal discrepancy\n\n   Key limitations:\n   - Translation to time domain requires careful inverse transformation\n   - Qualitative assessment of spectral features still needed\n   - Parametrization may miss non-periodic components\n\n   If the optimal solution has any form of periodicity, spectral optimization should yield substantial improvement. Even if not, the combined multi-scale exploration increases chances for global optimum.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Representation: Functions represented in 4 spaces:\n     T(time): Piecewise constant ('step') function on [0,2] with integral 1\n     F(frequency): Fourier coefficients f_k of the step function\n     S(spectral features): Selected frequency features g_k = |f_k|^2 + ph_k\n     I(inverse transform): Mapping from frequency space back to time space\n\n   Variables: \n     f: Fourier coefficients (C_n coefficients) to be optimized\n     g: Selected spectral features (K_n chosen coefficients)\n     h: Inverse transform (step function)\n\n   Exact objective: \n     C_5 = (2/N) * max_{k\u2208Z} || h(x) * (1-h(x+k)) ||_2 dx\n   In transformed form:\n     C_5 = (2/N) * max_k | \u27e8f_k * conj(f_{k+r})\u27e9 |\u00b2 // (with appropriate transforms)\n\n   Constraints:\n     0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n     \u222b_0\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\n   Optimization formulation:\n     Minimize: 1/N * max_k ||S_k(f)\u2016^2\n     Subject to:\n        h(x) \u2208 {step function} \n        \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n\n   solver: SpectralGradientDescent with analytic frequency gradient projection\n\n   Feasibility: Projected gradient with spectral normalization\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Step 1: Initial construction\n     If parent available: start at n=1280 samples\n     Else: use binary representation (threshold) at n=512 for start\n\n   Step 2: Spectral initialization\n     Construct Fourier transform of time-domain function\n     Select a subset (1/10) of square-magnitude coefficients\n     Optimize spectral features using projected gradient\n\n   Step 3: Multi-scale optimization\n     For scales s=0.1, 0.2, ..., 1.0 in frequency space:\n        Start with previous scale solution\n        Optimize \u03c6_k = phase adjustments\n        Optimize \u03c9_k = frequency weights\n        Compute time-domain inversion\n\n   Step 4: Re-projection and verification\n     Convert candidate to time domain\n     Project to ensure integrality and boundary conditions\n     Re-compute exact C_5\n\n   Step 5: Candidate assessment\n     Verify 0\u2264h_i\u22641 and sum=1280/2\n     Compare C_5 across candidates\n     If new best, update reference\n\n   Operations\n     \u2022 Fourier transform: scipy.fft.rfft for real-valued inputs\n     \u2022 Spectral selection: Uniform sampling across magnitude spectrum\n     \u2022 Phase adjustments: Unit modulus conservation\n     \u2022 Inversion: scipy.fft.irfft\n\n   Example implementation for gradient calculation:\n\n   def spectral_loss(f):\n        # f: complex Fourier coefficients\n        s = []\n        for k in selected_indices:\n            s_k = np.abs(f[k])**2 + ph_k*f[k].imag   // complex spectral feature\n            s.append(s_k)\n        M = np.max(s)\n        return (M / n)   # M scaled by dx=2/n\n\n   4. Search choices and variations\n   Primary search configuration:\n     spectral_dim = int(min(600, 0.9*N))\n     max_clip = 1.5   # maximal clip factor during gradient\n     ph_phases = 16   # phase adjustments per scale\n     grad_step = 0.05\n     ft_samples = 8   # frequency samples for initial selection\n\n   Adaptive triggers:\n     If stagnation (10 no-improve cycles): increase grad_step by 20%\n     If correlation exceed 0.381: switch to more aggressive phase search\n     If multiple phases stabilize: reduce optimization dimension\n\n   Example variations to try:\n     Variation A: Use wavelet coefficients as alternatives to Fourier\n        May uncover non-periodic handlers\n     Variation B: Include harmonic balancing (\u03c8_{m+n} = e^{2\u03c0i(m+n)} )\n        Could find additive structures\n     Variation C: Use radial basis functions during projection\n        Better preserve spatial consistency\n\n   5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Dominant cost: \n     FT operations: O(n log n) per candidate\n     Optimization: Scales with (spectral_dim)^2 * number_of_evaluations\n\n   Budget allocation:\n     Phase 1: n=256, time\u2248100s\n     Phase 2: n=512, time\u2248500s\n     Phase 3: n=1024, time\u2248700s\n\n   Termination conditions:\n     Time limit: When next doubling would exceed 600s remaining\n     Convergence: Same candidate 8x in a row with margin <1e-5\n     Fallback: Keep best stable candidate from n=512\n\n   Response to simulation issues:\n     NaN values: Clip phase to [-\u03c0,\u03c0] and resample\n     Boundary issues: Use periodic smoothing if needed\n     Overflow: Lower spectral_dim and adjust ph_phases\n\n   Fallback structure:\n     Maintain 3.Repositories: binary_init, wave_optim, phase_random\n\n     Search hierarchy:\n       0. wave_optim     (main method)\n       1. binary_init    (fallback)\n       2. phase_random   (least reliable)\n\n     Termination: Return most recently stable candidate\n\n   6. Validation and return contract\n   Feasibility checks:\n     1. All h_i \u2208 [0,1]\n     2. \u2211h_i = requested_length/2, tolerance 1e-8\n     3. np.angle(\u03c6_k) near zero (if complex)\n     4. FT checks for spectral consistency\n\n   Objective calculation:\n     Re-compute correlation with np.correlate in mode='full'\n\n   Metrics for comparison:\n     C_5_new = correlation_value\n     N_steps = n_points\n     Reduction = current_best - C_5_new\n\n   Return only if:\n     - Candidate passes all feasibility tests\n     - C_5 has improved in strict terms\n     - Time budget allows brief validation\n\n   Smoke tests:\n     Test_small_n = np.array([0,1,0,1,0,1]).astype(float)\n     assert 0 <= Test_small_n <= 1\n     Test_compute = compute_c5(Test_small_n)\n     print(f\"Check: computed C_5 = {Test_compute} (should be around 0.25 for length 8)\")\n\n   Example validation code (pseudocode):\n     def validate(h, n):\n        dx = 2.0/n\n        # Check boundaries\n        if min(h) < 0 or max(h) > 1: raise ValueError\n        if abs(sum(h)-n/2) > 1e-4: raise ValueError\n        corr_result = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n        max_corr = np.max(corr_result)\n        return (max_corr * dx, n)",
